The formula for linear regression is:
y = mx + b
where y is the dependent variable, x is the independent variable, m is the slope, and b is the y-intercept. 
To apply linear regression in C# to predict future outcomes based on past data, you can use a machine learning library such as ML.NET. ML.NET provides a Linear Regression trainer that can be used to train a model on past data and then use the model to make predictions on new data.
Feature engineering can be used to transform raw data into meaningful features for the model. This can involve techniques such as scaling, normalization, and one-hot encoding. For example, if you have a dataset with a categorical variable such as "color" that has values of "red", "green", and "blue", you can use one-hot encoding to transform this variable into three binary variables: "color_red", "color_green", and "color_blue".
The formula for hyperparameter tuning depends on the specific machine learning algorithm being used. In general, hyperparameter tuning involves selecting the best values for the hyperparameters of the model. This can be done using techniques such as grid search or random search.
Here is an example of how to implement linear regression in C# using ML.NET:
```csharp
using Microsoft.ML;
using Microsoft.ML.Data;
using System;
namespace LinearRegressionExample
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Create a new MLContext
            var context = new MLContext();
            // Define the data schema
            var schema = context.Data.SchemaDefinition.Create(typeof(DataPoint));
            // Load the training data
            var data = context.Data.LoadFromTextFile<DataPoint>("data.csv", schema: schema, hasHeader: true, separatorChar: ',');
            // Split the data into training and testing sets
            var (trainData, testData) = context.Data.TrainTestSplit(data, testFraction: 0.2);
            // Define the pipeline
            var pipeline = context.Transforms.Concatenate("Features", nameof(DataPoint.X))
                .Append(context.Transforms.NormalizeMinMax("Features"))
                .Append(context.Transforms.NormalizeMeanVariance("Features"))
                .Append(context.Transforms.Concatenate("Features", nameof(DataPoint.X), "Features"))
                .Append(context.Transforms.Concatenate("Label", nameof(DataPoint.Y)))
                .Append(context.Transforms.NormalizeMinMax("Label"))
                .Append(context.Transforms.NormalizeMeanVariance("Label"))
                .Append(context.Transforms.Concatenate("Label", nameof(DataPoint.Y), "Label"))
                .Append(context.Regression.Trainers.Sdca());
            // Train the model
            var model = pipeline.Fit(trainData);
            // Evaluate the model on the test data
            var predictions = model.Transform(testData);
            var metrics = context.Regression.Evaluate(predictions);
            Console.WriteLine($"R^2: {metrics.RSquared}");
            // Make a prediction on new data
            var predictionEngine = context.Model.CreatePredictionEngine<DataPoint, Prediction>(model);
            var prediction = predictionEngine.Predict(new DataPoint { X = 10 });
            Console.WriteLine($"Prediction: {prediction.Y}");
        }
    }
    public class DataPoint
    {
        [LoadColumn(0)]
        public float X { get; set; }
        [LoadColumn(1)]
        public float Y { get; set; }
    }
    public class Prediction
    {
        [ColumnName("Score")]
        public float Y { get; set; }
    }
}
```
In this example, we load a dataset from a CSV file, split it into training and testing sets, define a pipeline that includes feature engineering and linear regression, train the model on the training data, evaluate the model on the testing data, and make a prediction on new data. The feature engineering steps include concatenating the X variable with normalized and standardized versions of itself, and concatenating the Y variable with a normalized and standardized version of itself. The hyperparameters of the linear regression model are optimized automatically by the SDCA trainer.

