```java
public class AlphabetSong {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String alphabet = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ";
        String song = "";

        for (int i = 0; i < alphabet.length(); i++) {
            char letter = alphabet.charAt(i);
            String line = letter + "";

            if (letter == 'A') {
                line += ", " + letter + " " + letter;
            } else {
                line += ", " + letter;
            }

            line += ", " + letter + " " + letter + " " + letter;
            song += line + "\n";
        }

        System.out.println(song);
    }
}
```
In this code, we first declare a String variable `alphabet` that contains all the letters of the English alphabet. We also declare an empty String variable `song` that we will use to store the complete lyrics of the song.
We then use a `for` loop to iterate through each letter in the `alphabet` String. For each letter, we create a `line` String variable that contains the letter itself and the appropriate lyrics for that letter. We use an `if` statement to handle the special case of the letter "A", which has a slightly different set of lyrics.
Finally, we concatenate each `line` variable to the `song` variable, separated by a newline character, and print the final `song` variable to the console.
